WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.502
Unsafe API usage in C rarely exists because a team intentionally wanted fragile code.

2
00:00:05.502 --> 00:00:07.832
It usually survives through history.

3
00:00:07.832 --> 00:00:10.615
Large codebases carry old examples forward.

4
00:00:10.615 --> 00:00:14.176
Portability habits persist long after platforms change.

5
00:00:14.176 --> 00:00:18.577
Older coding standards may have treated certain functions as normal.

6
00:00:18.577 --> 00:00:25.632
A developer under schedule pressure may copy a familiar pattern without revisiting the assumptions behind it.

7
00:00:25.632 --> 00:00:31.523
Some C APIs are risky because they do not require the caller to provide a destination size.

8
00:00:31.523 --> 00:00:42.203
Others make truncation hard to notice, depend on null-terminated input without proving that termination exists, or return information that callers frequently ignore.

9
00:00:42.203 --> 00:00:45.375
The dangerous part is not only the function name.

10
00:00:45.375 --> 00:00:48.546
It is the easy path toward unchecked assumptions.

11
00:00:48.546 --> 00:00:53.530
Replacing an unsafe API is therefore not just a mechanical name substitution.

12
00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:55.990
The surrounding data contract matters.

13
00:00:55.990 --> 00:01:02.528
A call site has a source, a destination, a length expectation, an ownership rule, and a failure path.

14
00:01:02.528 --> 00:01:10.230
If those details are unclear, a safer-looking replacement can preserve the same bug or introduce a new behavior change.

15
00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:17.156
A safer replacement still needs correct buffer sizes, return-value checks, and deliberate failure handling.

16
00:01:17.156 --> 00:01:21.817
A bounded function called with the wrong bound is not safer in practice.

17
00:01:21.817 --> 00:01:30.749
A formatting function that reports truncation is only useful if the caller checks that report and decides what the program should do next.

18
00:01:30.749 --> 00:01:37.287
The goal of migration is to improve both security and reliability while preserving intended behavior.

19
00:01:37.287 --> 00:01:48.161
That means understanding normal inputs, boundary cases, old compatibility assumptions, and what should happen when the new code rejects something the old code accepted.

20
00:01:48.161 --> 00:01:53.145
Good API replacement is careful engineering, not a find-and-replace exercise.
